# Keep the build context small and, more importantly, keep secrets out of the image.
#
# Anything listed here can never end up in a layer, so this is the place .env has to be. An .env is
# excluded twice over — `docker build` would otherwise COPY it into the builder stage, where it would
# persist in that layer's history even if a later stage deleted it.

# Secrets. Runtime configuration is injected as environment variables, never baked in.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Build output. Rebuilt inside the image; a host .next is architecture- and platform-specific and
# would be wrong for the builder stage anyway.
.next
node_modules
*.tsbuildinfo

# Version control and tooling state
.git
.gitignore
.github
.opencode
.dockerignore
Dockerfile
docker-compose*.yml
README.md

# Local data and editor noise
.data
.vscode
.idea
*.log
.DS_Store

# Nothing in the image runs a browser. Playwright's binaries are downloaded by npm ci, not committed,
# and the E2E suites run on a workstation — but its config is still not needed to build or serve.
playwright.config.*
tests
e2e
